WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:06.205
Valjean arrived as the rebels were debating which of the five men should get the four uniforms.

2
00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:10.394
"Someone can have my uniform," Valjean volunteered.

3
00:00:10.394 --> 00:00:14.959
"Who is that man?" asked Courfeyrac. "How did he get in here?"

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:23.118
"I know him," said Marius. "His name is Fauchelevent." Marius' response told the men that Valjean was on their side.

5
00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:30.189
"The guard let me pass," said Valjean. "He must have thought that just one National Guard was not a threat."

6
00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:40.269
"Welcome, fellow citizen," said Enjolras. "Your actions will save one man's life, but do you realize we're all going to die in here?"

7
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:46.943
Valjean did not respond; he was busy exchanging clothes with the man who would wear his uniform.

8
00:00:46.944 --> 00:00:52.586
It was rapidly growing light as the rebels prepared for the attack on their barricade.

9
00:00:52.587 --> 00:00:55.006
They did not have long to wait.

10
00:00:55.007 --> 00:01:02.333
Soon a huge cannon appeared at the end of the street. Smoke rose from the cannon as the fuse was lit.

11
00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:11.824
"Fire!" ordered Enjolras. The rebel gunners fired at the cannon. The air filled with thick smoke and a terrible explosion from the cannon.

12
00:01:11.825 --> 00:01:17.817
When the smoke cleared, the rebels saw that not a single man in the barricade was hurt.

13
00:01:17.818 --> 00:01:21.403
"Bravo for the gunners!" shouted Courfeyrac.

14
00:01:21.404 --> 00:01:28.229
The big cannon fired at the barricade again. At the same moment, Gavroche tumbled into the barricade.

15
00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.547
His appearance there caused more shock than the cannon did.

16
00:01:31.548 --> 00:01:38.889
"What are you doing here?" cried Marius. "Who told you to come back? Did you put my letter in Cosette's hands?"

17
00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:43.293
Gavroche knew that he would be in trouble if he told the truth.

18
00:01:43.294 --> 00:01:48.555
"She was sleeping, so I left it with the porter. She'll get it when she wakes up."

19
00:01:48.556 --> 00:01:52.888
Meanwhile Enjolras was dealing with a hole in the barricade.

20
00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:56.372
"We need a mattress to deaden the effect of the cannon."

21
00:01:56.373 --> 00:02:03.092
"Look up there," said Courfeyrac. Someone had hung a mattress in front of his windows to keep them from breaking.

22
00:02:03.093 --> 00:02:05.618
"But how will we get the mattress down?"

23
00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:13.189
Valjean had been sitting in a corner, but now he sprang to his feet and took careful aim at the mattress.

24
00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:20.364
With two shots, he cut the ropes that held it. The mattress fell to the street outside the barricade.

25
00:02:20.365 --> 00:02:28.332
Without waiting for instructions, he clambered over the top of the barricade, seized the mattress, and brought it back inside.

26
00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:34.673
A cheer went up from the rebels, and Enjolras rushed to shake Valjean's hand.

27
00:02:34.674 --> 00:02:39.102
"You're a hero of the republic!" exclaimed Enjolras.

28
00:02:39.103 --> 00:02:41.822
The assault on the barricade continued.

29
00:02:41.823 --> 00:02:48.165
The attackers hoped that those inside would soon run out of ammunition, and indeed that was happening.

30
00:02:48.166 --> 00:02:54.033
Marius suddenly spotted someone outside the barricade gathering bullets from dead soldiers.

31
00:02:54.034 --> 00:03:00.498
It was Gavroche, who had taken a basket from the tavern and was quietly going about this task.

32
00:03:00.499 --> 00:03:06.395
"Gavroche! What are you doing out there?" exclaimed Marius. "You'll be killed!"

33
00:03:06.396 --> 00:03:12.308
"Stop calling to him!" hissed Courfeyrac. "You're only drawing attention to him."

34
00:03:12.309 --> 00:03:18.292
They watched in horror and fascination as Gavroche danced around the incoming bullets.

35
00:03:18.293 --> 00:03:27.562
"It's raining hard out here this morning!" Gavroche teased the attackers. "You'll have to do better than that! You'll never hit—"

36
00:03:27.563 --> 00:03:34.864
At that moment Gavroche was struck by a bullet. Marius sprang out of the barricade with Courfeyrac on his heels.

37
00:03:34.865 --> 00:03:38.818
But it was too late; Gavroche had been killed.

38
00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:44.358
Courfeyrac brought back the basket of ammunition while Marius carried Gavroche's body.

39
00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:48.857
A bullet grazed Marius' forehead, but he hardly noticed.

40
00:03:48.858 --> 00:03:57.927
"Poor Gavroche," thought Marius. "His father saved my father's life. If only I'd been able to do the same for him."

41
00:03:57.928 --> 00:04:03.102
Back inside, the rebels feverishly worked to build up the barricade.

42
00:04:03.103 --> 00:04:12.268
"Get this spy out of the way!" Enjolras pointed at Javert, who was still tied to a post. "The last man alive should kill him."

43
00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:20.457
"Excuse me," said Valjean. "You called me a hero earlier. Might I have the honor of killing the spy now?"

44
00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:21.896
"Go ahead!"

45
00:04:21.897 --> 00:04:27.782
Someone was cleaning Marius' wound when Valjean untied Javert from the post.

46
00:04:27.783 --> 00:04:33.378
As Valjean led the prisoner away, Marius suddenly recognized him.

47
00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:38.028
"That's the policeman who gave me the pistol! He's a spy?"

48
00:04:38.029 --> 00:04:44.154
While Marius was pondering this, Valjean took Javert to an alleyway leading out of the barricade.

49
00:04:44.155 --> 00:04:50.774
Neither man said a word to the other. Valjean stopped and looked Javert in the eye.

50
00:04:50.775 --> 00:04:54.916
"Do you recognize me now?" asked Valjean.

51
00:04:54.917 --> 00:05:02.679
"Of course." Javert gave a rueful smile. "I suppose it's appropriate that my life should end this way."

52
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:08.750
Valjean took a knife and cut the last of Javert's bonds. "You're free to go."

53
00:05:08.751 --> 00:05:14.600
Javert was not easily astonished, but he stood there speechless.

54
00:05:14.601 --> 00:05:19.838
"I don't expect to get out of here alive," Valjean continued.

55
00:05:19.839 --> 00:05:29.735
"But if I do, I want you to know where to find me. I go by the name Fauchelevent and live at 7 Rue de l'Homme Armé."

56
00:05:29.736 --> 00:05:37.409
Javert shook his head in disbelief. "This isn't the way that things are supposed to end. I'd rather you killed me."

57
00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.409
"Just get out of here before I change my mind!"

58
00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:46.566
Javert slowly walked away. When he was out of sight, Valjean fired a shot.

59
00:05:46.567 --> 00:05:50.893
Marius heard the shot. "Javert is dead," he thought.

60
00:05:50.894 --> 00:05:58.639
And now the serious battle began. A massive column of soldiers entered the street and began firing upon the barricade.

61
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.054
They swarmed over the top of the barricade and fought with the students and workers.

62
00:06:04.055 --> 00:06:10.044
Courfeyrac was killed, and so were many others. Enjolras remained unhurt.

63
00:06:10.045 --> 00:06:14.536
Marius fought on, his head covered with wounds.

64
00:06:14.537 --> 00:06:19.369
Suddenly a bullet pierced his shoulder, and he felt himself falling.

65
00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:27.115
"I'll be taken prisoner now, and shot," he thought. As he passed out, his last thought was of Cosette.

66
00:06:27.116 --> 00:06:33.983
Seconds later Marius was indeed taken prisoner, but his captor was not a French soldier.

67
00:06:33.984 --> 00:06:39.345
Instead Valjean picked up Marius and carried him into a corner.

68
00:06:39.346 --> 00:06:46.107
There the older man stopped and let Marius slide to the ground. Their situation was grim.

69
00:06:46.108 --> 00:06:54.083
They would be safe for a few more minutes, but the battle raged all around them. They'd never escape this massacre.

70
00:06:54.084 --> 00:06:59.662
Valjean looked at the barricade in front of them. A row of soldiers' bayonets sat above it.

71
00:06:59.663 --> 00:07:04.757
The tavern was behind Valjean and Marius, but they could get trapped in there.

72
00:07:04.758 --> 00:07:09.648
Valjean stared at the street, hoping an answer would come to him.

73
00:07:09.649 --> 00:07:14.571
Then suddenly he saw it: a metal grate covering an opening in the street.

74
00:07:14.572 --> 00:07:20.981
Valjean pulled up the grate, loaded Marius onto his back, and lowered himself into the opening.

75
00:07:20.982 --> 00:07:25.156
Below, everything around them was completely dark.

76
00:07:25.157 --> 00:07:30.243
There was deep peace, absolute silence, night.

77
00:07:30.244 --> 00:07:35.736
It was not unlike the time Valjean had carried Cosette over the wall and into the convent.

78
00:07:35.737 --> 00:07:39.973
Above their heads, he could hear troops storming the tavern.

79
00:07:39.974 --> 00:07:49.037
At first Valjean could see nothing in the blackness. He reached out one hand and then the other to touch the walls around him.

80
00:07:49.038 --> 00:07:56.196
The ground below his feet was solid. Where were they? A foul smell gave Valjean the answer.

81
00:07:56.197 --> 00:08:03.911
For the moment they were safe in the sewers below the streets of Paris. But how would they get out?

